[Memory: owner_tension]
@purpose Classify design tensions: CONTRADICTION (zero-sum) vs ORTHOGONAL (area expansion possible).
@date 2026-02-17
@source challenge.md, product_context.atc, concept_harness.md, requires/list.md, embedded_cpp_check/SKILL.md

[Legend]
  - ORTHOGONAL: Two independent axes. Both can improve simultaneously with right design.
                The work is "expanding the area of the rectangle."
  - CONTRADICTION: Same axis, opposite directions. Gain on one side is loss on the other.
                   Requires a hard decision or an acceptance of a fixed ratio.

[T1: Abstraction vs. Performance]
  axes: (code modularity / testability) vs (call overhead / memory cost)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — RESOLVED by Concept Harness
  evidence:
    - vtable: 3-5 cycles + 8B/object → Concept Harness: 0 cycles + 0B (@see concept_harness.md §パフォーマンス比較)
    - Full abstraction (IoC, DI, mock injection) AND zero runtime cost.
    - The rectangle is already maximized on both axes.
  risk: none. This tension is closed.

[T2: Host Simplicity vs. Trap Overhead (fd_write)]
  axes: (host code size → 15KLOC budget) vs (execution speed → trap count)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — measurable, and latency-tolerant
  reasoning:
    - These are independent axes: code size and trap latency.
    - Shim-loop: small host, more traps. Host-loop: larger host, fewer traps.
    - CRITICAL INSIGHT: fd_write is an I/O path, not a hot loop.
      UART @115200bps: 87µs/byte. Trap cost: ~few µs. I/O dominates.
      Even 5 traps per scatter-gather vanish into UART latency.
    - Question is not "how fast" but "is slow acceptable here?" — YES.
  fast_verification:
    - vSoC standalone harness (Phase1 artifact) + DWT->CYCCNT on Cortex-M
    - Instrument fireball_call entry/exit with cycle counter
    - 1 trap cost becomes a hard number in < 1 hour of measurement
    - No need for full system integration to answer this question
  action: measure trap cost in standalone harness → confirm I/O-dominance → shim-loop wins.
  @see challenge.md §1

[T3: Safety vs. Performance (vMMIO access validation)]
  axes: (memory safety → validate every guest access) vs (throughput → skip checks)
  verdict: ABSORBED ✅ — unified vMMIO model eliminates the trade-off
  reasoning:
    - Original framing: separate vsoc_validate_ptr on every syscall buffer = per-call cost.
    - New reality: ALL host-guest access goes through vMMIO permission table.
      GPIO, shared memory, syscall buffers — same path, same single check.
    - Permission table lookup IS the validation. No separate mechanism.
    - Permitted addresses: zero additional cost (direct physical access).
    - Unpermitted addresses: rejected (trap). No partial check, no gradual degradation.
    - The “trade-off” dissolves because there is no alternative path that skips the check.
      The check IS the architecture, not an optional safety layer.
  resolution: ABSORBED into vMMIO design. Not a tension anymore.
  @see challenge.md §2, vmmio.md §1

[T4: Latency vs. Fairness (CSP Handoff)]
  axes: (communication latency → direct switch) vs (scheduling fairness → round-robin)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — resolved by approximate yield
  reasoning:
    - Handoff improves latency on one axis.
    - Approximate yield (trace-count-based forced yield) independently maintains fairness.
    - Both mechanisms operate on different timescales and don't interfere.
    - Formal verification (ipc_handoff.tla) confirmed no deadlock.
  risk: low. Yield threshold tuning is needed but is a calibration task, not a design conflict.
  @see challenge.md §4

[T5: Memory Cost vs. Scheduling Efficiency (BLOCKED list)]
  axes: (RAM usage → +pointer per task_t) vs (scheduler scan time → skip blocked tasks)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — quantifiable trade
  reasoning:
    - Cost: 4 bytes × max_tasks. With 16 tasks = 64 bytes. Negligible vs 64KB.
    - Benefit: O(ready) instead of O(all) per schedule tick.
    - These are independent: memory cost is static, scheduling benefit scales with blocked ratio.
    - The "INTERRUPTED flag + deferred unlink" avoids ISR atomicity issues.
  risk: low. The 64-byte cost is well within budget.
  @see challenge.md §3

[T6: 15KLOC Budget vs. Feature Coverage]
  axes: (code size constraint) vs (number of features / subsystems)
  verdict: CONDITIONAL ORTHOGONAL ✅⚠️ — depends on design simplicity
  reasoning:
    - Naive view: every feature adds lines → zero-sum → CONTRADICTION.
    - Refined view: code size = f(features × complexity_per_feature).
      If complexity_per_feature is low (few branches, few special cases),
      then features can grow while code stays small.
    - Design simplicity is the THIRD VARIABLE that decouples the two axes.
      @inv simple_design (few case-splits) ⇒ size ∧ features both improve
    - This makes T6 orthogonal CONDITIONAL on maintaining design simplicity.
  condition:
    - complexity_per_feature must remain low.
    - Indicators of violation: deeply nested if/switch, many special-case handlers,
      growing glue code between components.
    - If complexity creeps up, T6 reverts to CONTRADICTION.
  mitigation:
    - Code generation from WIT reduces boilerplate (doesn't add to authored KLOC).
    - Shim library (guest-side) doesn't count against host 15KLOC.
    - Concept Harness avoids vtable/wrapper code bloat.
    - Single-trap design (fireball_call) eliminates per-syscall dispatch code.
  risk: MEDIUM. Must be continuously monitored.
         Phase0-Gate criterion 3 (size verification) is the checkpoint.
  @see requires/list.md {Size_15KLOC}

[T7: Static Allocation vs. Runtime Flexibility]
  axes: (no heap / no dynamic alloc) vs (handle varying workloads / task counts)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — resolved by partition pattern
  reasoning:
    - Partitioned memory (bump allocator, static pools) gives runtime flexibility
      WITHOUT dynamic allocation.
    - Task count is bounded (constexpr). Buffer sizes are bounded (constexpr).
    - Flexibility is achieved through parameterization, not dynamism.
  risk: low, as long as partition sizes are correctly derived from invariants.
  @see embedded_cpp_check/SKILL.md §メモリ管理パターン

[T8: Formal Specification Cost vs. Development Velocity]
  axes: (WIT/TLA+ rigor → upfront time) vs (time-to-implementation → move fast)
  verdict: ORTHOGONAL ✅ — different time horizons
  reasoning:
    - Short-term: formalism costs more time upfront.
    - Long-term: formalism eliminates rework, catches design bugs before implementation.
    - These axes are orthogonal because they operate on different timescales.
    - The Bonsai workflow explicitly manages this: invest in design, harvest in implementation.
  risk: the investment is FRONT-LOADED. If Phase0 runs too long, Phase1 gets compressed.
        But this is a scheduling risk, not a design contradiction.
  @see product_context.atc [Physical-Time-Model]

[Summary]
  ORTHOGONAL (resolved):              T1, T3, T4, T5, T7      — 5 items
  ORTHOGONAL (measurable/verifiable): T2, T8                   — 2 items
  CONDITIONAL ORTHOGONAL:             T6 (iff design simplicity held) — 1 item
  CONTRADICTION:                      NONE

  @inv No genuine contradictions remain in the design.
  @inv T6 is the critical watch item: if complexity creeps, it becomes contradiction.
  @inv T3 was absorbed by the unified vMMIO model — the check IS the architecture.
  @note Design simplicity is not an aesthetic preference — it is a load-bearing structural constraint.
